    "content": "Acceleration Consortium 2025     \n\n[![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6935f8052c49ab4956e4b162/6953fb4a66bb7396b9747d9e_618bf0cd61c333a5774478d3_Acceleration%20Consortium_Reverse%20U%20of%20T%20Lock-Up_Digital%201.png)](/)\n\nAbout\n\n[\n\nVision\n\nOur plans for a materially better future\n\n\n\n](/vision)[\n\nTeam\n\nOur team at the Acceleration Consortium\n\n\n\n](/team)[\n\nCareers\n\nOpen roles and opportunities\n\n\n\n](/jobs)[\n\nEDI\n\nOur comprehensive equity, diversity and inclusion program\n\n\n\n](/edi)[\n\nContact\n\nGet in touch\n\n\n\n](/get-in-touch)\n\nResearch\n\n[\n\nAccelerated Discovery\n\nHow self-driving labs work and their potential to change the world\n\n\n\n](/what-is-an-sdl)[\n\nLabs\n\nOur core facilities and related labs\n\n\n\n](/ac-labs-and-facilities)[\n\nPublications\n\nAC-related research and discoveries\n\n\n\n](/publications)[\n\nCommunity Store\n\nCustom tools and support for self-driving research.\n\n\n\n](/category/equipment)\n\nConsortium\n\n[\n\nFull Members\n\nOur researchers around the world\n\n\n\n](/members/members)[\n\nAffiliate Members\n\nOur affiliate researchers around the world\n\n\n\n](/members/affiliate-members)[\n\nFellows\n\nOur network of postdoctoral fellows\n\n\n\n](/members/fellows)[\n\nPartners\n\nOur community of government, industry, startups, and not-for-profits\n\n\n\n](/members/partners)\n\nPrograms\n\n[\n\nPrograms\n\nPrograms and grants to support the next generation of scientists\n\n\n\n](/programs)[\n\nEvents\n\nOur upcoming workshops, seminars, and more\n\n\n\n](/events)[\n\nAccelerate Conference 2026\n\nOur annual conference\n\n\n\n](https://ai4x.cc/)[\n\nMicrocredentials\n\nHands-on courses to learn about the principles and tools of accelerated discovery\n\n\n\n](https://learn.utoronto.ca/programs-courses/certificates/autonomous-systems-discovery)\n\nNews\n\n[\n\nNews and Media\n\nThe latest at the AC and in the field of accelerated discovery\n\n\n\n](/news)[\n\nImpact\n\nHow the AC is making a mark\n\n\n\n](/impact)[\n\nFAQ\n\nAnswers to commonly asked questions\n\n\n\n](/frequently-asked-questions)\n\n[\n\nJoin the Consortium\n\n\n\n\n\n](/join-consortium)[\n\nButton\n\n\n\n\n\n](/search)\n\n[\n\nCart\n\n0\n\n](#)\n\n#### Your Cart\n\n-   :\n\n[\n\nRemove\n\n](#)\n\nSubtotal\n\nPay with browser.\n\n[Continue to Checkout](/checkout)\n\nNo items found.\n\nProduct is not available in this quantity.\n\nSep 1, 2026\n\n# AC-U of T startup tackles the growing challenge of cooling powerful technologies\n\nArticle\n\n## Highlights\n\n.01\n\nAuthor\n\n.02\n\nPublish Date\n\nSep 1, 2026\n\n.03\n\nCategory\n\n\n\nGeneral\n\n.04\n\nShare\n\n[](#)[](#)\n\nBacked by the Acceleration Consortium, FL01 uses self-driving lab (SDL) technology to test potential new cooling fluids 100 times faster than industry methods\n\n![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/plugins/Basic/assets/placeholder.60f9b1840c.svg)\n\nA new AI-driven startup founded at the University of Toronto and supported by the Acceleration Consortium (AC) is heating up the tech space by helping to cool down energy-intensive technologies and infrastructure.\n\nLaunched in November 2025, FL01 has developed patented sensor technology to accelerate the discovery of coolants for heat management in battery packs, high-power electronics and data centres, among other applications.  \n\n“Our sensors work 100 times faster to test and measure the thermophysical, or heat-related properties, of potential new fluids than the methods currently used in industry,” says Mohammed Zargartalebi, FL01’s chief executive officer and U of T research associate. “These sensors are part of our self-driving lab (SDL), which can reduce research and development timelines from years to weeks.”\n\nCooling fluids help transfer heat and are essential to industrial processes across multiple sectors, including energy, electronics, transportation and manufacturing. As the demand for processing power in technology increases, finding new, more sustainable, high-performing cooling fluids that enable precise temperature control could help industries operate more efficiently and produce safer products.  \n\nZargartalebi says FL01 has attracted early interest from several multinational technology and energy companies that require increasingly powerful electrical systems, but face cooling challenges.  \n\n“Conventional cooling systems and liquids cannot respond to the growing demands of emerging technologies, which are generating heat faster than it can be removed,” he says. “It can take these big companies five to 10 years to develop new fluids, which is where we think we can offer a rapid solution.”  \n\nWhile FL01’s technological innovation lies in the scale and speed of its self-driving lab’s testing capabilities, Zargartalebi says the data generated by the technology may ultimately prove most valuable.  \n\n“The information our technology is generating is unprecedented,” he says. “Data on thermal fluids has been slow to collect because of manual testing, with an estimated 4,000 publicly available data points collected over the last 100 years. One of our devices can generate 10,000 data points in two months.”\n\nFL01 commercializes research that began in 2022 at the Sinton Lab, led by David Sinton, a professor in the Department of Mechanical Engineering and Industrial Engineering at U of T.  \n\nSinton and the multidisciplinary lab team of mechanical and chemical engineers use self-driving lab (SDL) technologies, including robotics and automation, that led to the spinout.  \n\nThe lab pursued commercialization with support from the Acceleration Consortium (AC), a major research initiative at U of T focused on the accelerated discovery of new materials and molecules.  \n\nThrough its programs, workshops and conferences, in addition to a global network of nearly 100 academic, industry and government partners, the AC helped FL01 tap into a broader startup community.\n\n“The AC has helped us learn more about SDL tools and technologies from researchers around the world and connected us with key industry partners and opportunities,” says Zargartalebi. “We’re grateful to be part of the ecosystem the AC has built.”  \n\nFL01 is currently prototyping its technology with several industry partners as it works toward commercial applications in thermal management and fluid testing and discovery.\n\nTo learn more about FL01, visit their website: [fl01.com](https://fl01.com)  \n\n*‍*\n\n*Thumbnail image: FL01’s self-driving lab (SDL) setup, which uses patented sensor technologies to measure and test potential new thermal fluids, including coolants. Photo Credit: FL01*\n\nRelated News\n\n[\n\n\n\nGeneral\n\n![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/693f2df283a54888f72af04c/6a96dcbdfbdde94e5b2b7b32_LUMILabImage.png)\n\nRead More\n\nU of T researchers use a self-driving lab to advance mRNA treatment for cystic fibrosis\n\n\n\n](/news/u-of-t-researchers-use-a-self-driving-lab-to-advance-mrna-treatment-for-cystic-fibrosis)\n\n[\n\n\n\nGeneral\n\n![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/693f2df283a54888f72af04c/6a96d6427f4e2e2b2162cd79_FL01%20SDL.png)\n\nRead More\n\nAC-U of T startup tackles the growing challenge of cooling powerful technologies\n\n\n\n](/news/ac-u-of-t-startup-tackles-the-growing-challenge-of-cooling-powerful-technologies)\n\n[\n\n\n\nGeneral\n\n![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/693f2df283a54888f72af04c/69a4fa86a8acacd292f0060e_lash-miller-interior-slide%20(1).jpg)\n\nRead More\n\nUniversity of Toronto breaks ground on new home for the Acceleration Consortium\n\n\n\n](/news/university-of-toronto-breaks-ground-on-new-home-for-the-acceleration-consortium)\n\n[\n\n\n\nGeneral\n\n![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/693f2df283a54888f72af04c/69a4f96afc2096d98ab8a84d_38-AccelerationConsortium.jpg)\n\nRead More\n\nU of T receives $200-million grant to support Acceleration Consortium's ‘self-driving labs’ research\n\n\n\n](/news/u-of-t-receives-200-million-grant-to-support-acceleration-consortiums-self-driving-labs-research)\n\n[\n\n\n\nGeneral\n\n![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/693f2df283a54888f72af04c/694897006b890b3cd070e3d5_66c397b9b406a64addd49374_2024-04-19-AC%2520Matter%2520Lab%2520Tour-ChrystiaFreeland-2_0%2520(2)%2520copy.jpeg)\n\nRead More\n\nDeputy prime minister meets with university leaders, researchers, grad students at U of T's Acceleration Consortium\n\n\n\n](/news/https-www-utoronto-ca-news-deputy-prime-minister-meets-university-leaders-researchers-grad-students-u-t-s-acceleration)\n\n[\n\n\n\nGeneral\n\n![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/693f2df283a54888f72af04c/69a5c4cbf7045f4074e6c9b8_laser4.jpg)\n\nRead More\n\nGift from Schmidt Futures to spark a revolution in AI-based STEM research at the University of Toronto\n\n\n\n](/news/gift-from-schmidt-futures-to-spark-a-revolution-in-ai-based-stem-research-at-the-university-of-toronto)\n\n[\n\n\n\nGeneral\n\n![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/693f2df283a54888f72af04c/69a4f9b6ace5625cf01bac56_37-AccelerationConsortium.jpg)\n\nRead More\n\nExperts say $200-million grant awarded to U of T will drive ‘big science’ via the Acceleration Consortium\n\n\n\n](/news/experts-say-200-million-grant-awarded-to-u-of-t-will-drive-big-science-via-the-acceleration-consortium)\n\n[\n\n\n\nGeneral\n\n![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/693f2df283a54888f72af04c/69a5cf698be64af378129164_star.jpg)\n\nRead More\n\nAcceleration Consortium’s Sean Caffrey receives President’s Excellence Award for Distinguished Excellence\n\n\n\n](/news/acceleration-consortiums-sean-caffrey-receives-presidents-excellence-award-for-distinguished-excellence)\n\n## Subscribe to our newsletter to get curated insights delivered straight to your inbox\n\n[\n\nSubscribe\n\n\n\n\n\n](https://accelerationconsortium.substack.com/)\n\n![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6935f8052c49ab4956e4b162/694b144fd7cddad9b6f46c21_canada-first.png)\n\n-   ### About\n    \n-   [Vision](/vi