    "role": "tool",
    "tool_call_id": "call_02_36zFHLf1KVyhNT7uC2Zo6442",
    "content": "Title: Accelerated fluid discovery for data center immersion cooling\nURL: https://doi.org/10.82417/pw8c-8z69\nPublished: 2025-10-21T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: Rezaei, Hanie, Ebrahimiazar, Maryam, Zargartalebi, Mohammad, Sinton, David\nHighlights:\n# Accelerated fluid discovery for data center immersion cooling\n\nEspace ÉTS (ETS). Published: 2025-10-21. 0 citations.\n\n## Authors\n\n- Rezaei, Hanie\n- Ebrahimiazar, Maryam\n- Zargartalebi, Mohammad\n- Sinton, David\n\n## Topics\n\n...\n\n# Paper Title (use style: paper title)\n\nProceedings of the Canadian Society for Mechanical Engineering International Congress 32nd Annual Conference of the Computational Fluid Dynamics Society of Canada Canadian Society of Rheology Symposium CSME-CFDSC-CSR 2025 May 25–28, 2025, Montréal, Québec, Canada\n\nHanie Rezaei 1, Maryam Ebrahimiazar 1, Mohammad Zargartalebi 1, David Sinton 1* 1 Mechanical & Industrial Engineering, University of Toronto, Toronto, Canada * dave.sinton@utoronto.ca\n\n## Abstract\n\nRapid expansion of data centers to support high-performance computing has amplified the demand for efficient thermal management solutions. Immersion cooling offers a promising approach, but it introduces new challenges in identifying a suitable coolant compatible with this technology. Along with optimized thermophysical properties, electrical properties and material compatibility are also critical considerations. This study focuses on developing a self-driving lab to accelerate the discovery of optimal coolants. An automated high-throughput AI-powered screening platform has been developed to analyze key properties, including viscosity, density, heat capacity, thermal conductivity, dielectric constant, and additive compatibility. The system analyzes up to five samples per hour, achieving a 100x higher throughput than conventional methods. A machine learning platform is first benchmarked using >1,000 data from ten liquids and their binary and ternary mixtures. The pure liquids include esters, alcohols, ketones, ethers, and aldehydes. These data points were gathered at 40 oC to ensure the liquids meet real-world operating conditions. The developed platform will be used to discover the optimum mixture from a large chemical space, guiding the next experiment in the fluid discovery loop. This integrated approach significantly reduces the number of required tests, leading to substantial time and material savings. This work advances the development and adoption of next-generation cooling technologies, addressing the increasing thermal management demand posed by high-performance computing in data centers.\n\n---\n\nTitle: What an inspiring week at the Accelerate Conference in Toronto! | Mahyar Rajabi Kochi\nURL: https://www.linkedin.com/posts/mahyar-rajabi-kochi-34b2521a1_accelerateconference-ai4chemistry-selfdrivinglab-activity-7361775950981672961-LIql\nPublished: 2025-08-14T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: Mahyar Rajabi Kochi\nHighlights:\nI was honoured to lead and be part of SDL projects with a focus on mixture modeling and discovery, including presenting our poster on FAMBO — Feature Adaptive Mixture Bayesian Optimization. FAMBO serves as a planner in our SDL, designed for mixture discovery for immersion cooling applications in next-gen electric vehicles battery systems. This was a collaborative effort between AI4ChemS and Professor David Sinton group, and I’m grateful to have worked alongside Mohammad Zargartalebi and Maryam Ebrahimiazar.\n\n---\n\nTitle: SDL5: Formulations\nURL: https://acceleration.utoronto.ca/sdls/sdl5-formulations\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n## Overview\n\nAutonomous formulation of materials comprising combinatorial mixing; formation of liquids, semi-solids, solids; characterization of structure and function, and application testing across personal care, chemical, healthcare, and other industries\n\n...\n\nFormulation is the process of combining ingredients, often familiar on their own, to create something with entirely new properties, similar to how a chef transforms basic staples into dishes with specific textures, tastes, and behaviours. But while the idea sounds simple, the reality is complex: even a small set of ingredients can be mixed in countless ways, with each ratio, processing step, and condition producing a different outcome. This combinatorial design space, regardless of the sector or application, means that finding the “right” formulation is rarely straightforward and often requires exploring an enormous number of possibilities. A well-designed formulation can make the difference between a life-saving drug and one that breaks down before it works, or between a crop treatment that protects plants and one that washes off in the first rain. The Formulations Self-Driving Lab (SDL5) combines advanced artificial intelligence, active learning, and flexible automation to cut through formulation complexity in ways traditional\n\n...\n\nA low-cost automated system to design and test thermal fluids for\n\n---\n\nTitle: Differential microthermometry enables high-throughput calorimetry\nURL: https://doi.org/10.1039/d3ee03865c\nPublished: 2023-12-20T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: Amin Kazemi, Mohammad Zargartalebi, David A. Sinton\nHighlights:\n## Abstract\n\nThe assessment of heat capacity, or calorimetry, is critical to the development of fluids that will cool electrified processes and systems, such as computing infrastructure, data centres, and high-performance electric vehicle batteries. However, the throughput of conventional measurement approaches is limited by the need to quantify heat flux. Here, we present an approach that is independent of heat flux quantification, eliminating the intrinsic bottleneck in calorimetry and enabling high-accuracy, high-speed fluid screening. The principle is the measurement of an advective temperature bias introduced by a sample fluid vs. a reference fluid, flowing in parallel symmetric microchannels. This microthermometric approach is tested in non-insulated flow-through microfluidic channels, demonstrating heat-flux independence while enabling continuous heat capacity measurement of a fluid stream. We assessed performance by measuring the heat capacity of 35 pure liquids and gases, liquid mixtures, and nanofluids, exhibiting a broad range of viscosities (0.015 -400 mPa•s), thermal conductivities (0.02 -0.6 W m -1 K -1 ), temperatures (5 -120 °C), and pressures (1 -70 bar). We achieve an accuracy of ±1.1%, matching that of the gold standard, with a throughput that exceeds the incumbent by two orders of magnitude (∼1 min vs. hours). This approach enables continuous and rapid screening in a high-throughput manner, enabling the accelerated discovery of energy-carrying fluids. Methods for accelerated discovery are transforming biology and pharmaceutical sciences, but these have yet to be applied in the energy industry in part due to low-throughput of traditional thermal property measurements. Heat capacity is a crucial thermophysical property in energy applications, and of particular importance to the challenge of cooling high-performance computing infrastructure, maintaining high-energy density batteries, and the electrification of industrial processes broadly. A new high-throughput approach has been developed to measure the specific heat capacity of fluids accurately and 100 times faster than past approaches. Based on a continuous flow-through microthermometric process, this method circumvents the challenge of heat flux measurement that has presented a barrier to past approaches, thereby enabling both accuracy and throughput. The new method has potential to accelerate discovery in thermal applications.\n\n...\n\nThermal fluids are central to the current energy system 1, 2 and to realizing the potential of industrial electrification, highpower computing, renewable energy generation, and energy storage. 3, 4 The demands of these applications are distinct from those of the internal combustion engines that have driven the development of coolants for over a century. 5 Addressing the emerging applications will require accelerated discovery of bespoke thermal fluid formulations. Methods for accelerated discovery have advanced greatlyparticularly in biology and pharmaceutical sciences -driven by the combination of low-volume, high-throughput measurements, automation, and machine intelligence. [6] [7] ] [8] However, this pace of discovery has not been matched in the energy sector, in part due to the high-volume, low-throughput nature of conventional thermophysical property measurements. [9] [10] ] [11] The energy-carrying capacity of a fluid is governed by the specific heat capacity ( ). Fluids with higher offer advantages such as more efficient cooling with reduced fluid volume, lower energy consumption for circulation, and enhanced temperature stability. 12 These attributes are especially crucial in the automotive and aviation industries, where the cooling liquid must be transported over long distances or durations. There is a further need to improve upon cooling fluids applicable to the direct cooling of electrical systems, including data centers and high-performance batteries. 13 Achieving the combination of thermally absorbing and electrically insulating properties, in addition to all the other metrics required of commercial working fluids, is an unmet challenge. 14, 15 The data is not readily available for many molecules, particularly the newly synthesized ones. Although computational methods can predict , their accuracy can be compromised by the need for molecular parameters 16 , such as molecular volume 17 . This limitation becomes even more pronounced for larger molecules and those with complex intermolecular interactions (e.g., hydrogen bonding). In addition, improving upon computational prediction capability will require high-quality input data for training and validation. The capabilities of computational and experimental methods are thus linked, as is the case in material discovery applications. 18, 19 The measurement of heat capacity, or calorimetry, is commonly achieved by introducing a known quantity of heat to a sample and recording the temperature change. 20 The conventional approach is the differential scanning calorimetry (DSC), where the heat given to the substances is measured by heat flux sensors or calculated by measuring the temperature difference along a conduction pathway via Fourier's law. The measurement accuracy relies on the minimization of uncontrolled modes of heat gain (e.g., Joule heating from sensors) 21 or heat loss (e.g., through the sample container or sensor wires). 22, 23 As a result, measured by different instruments can vary by up to ∼7% for the same compound. 24 The measurement is a batch process, involves many manual steps for calibration, sample weighing, loading, unloading, and cleaning, and is thus fundamentally low throughput (usually 3-4 hours per test). 25 icrofluidic devices offer the potential for flow-through testing with reduced sample volume and testing time. 26, 27 However, microfluidic approaches have found more success in measuring physical and chemical attributes, 28, 29 with comparatively little success in measuring thermal properties. The high surface-tovolume ratio inherent to small scales, 30, 31 is advantageous when controlling the physical or chemical environment, 32, 33 and disadvantageous when controlling the thermal environment -as heat is rapidly lost to the much larger thermal mass of the surrounding materials. 23, 34 This heat loss issue has been partly resolved by operating microfluidic channels in a vacuum environment. One such microfluidic calorimeter 35 employed an applied heat flux to assess heat capacity in vacuum, however, conductive and radiative heat losses persisted. [36] [37] 7] [38] [39] [40] [41] [42] [43] In all past cases, the measurement of small heat fluxes characteristic of microfluidic flows has limited accuracy. 20, 44 Despite the potential for high throughput calorimetry, the challenge of thermal management at small scales has limited the field. 21, 22 ere, we propose an alternative method to measure the specific heat capacity of fluids which is independent of heat flux quantification. The approach is to assess the advective temperature bias introduced by a sample fluid vs. a reference fluid, flowing in parallel symmetric microchannels under similar heat transfer conditions. Differential temperature measurement provides high resolution. Without the need to quantify or control heat loss, this approach is scaleindependent, and thus can be determined with both high accuracy and high throughput. The microscale flow-through approach offers additional advantages, including measurement at high pressure, high temperature, and in a continuous screening fashion applicable to accelerated material discovery workflows.\n\n...\n\nIn the proposed calorimeter, two identical flow channels transit between a hot and cold reservoir (Fig. 1a ). The advective heat transfer of the fluid influences the temperature field in each channel, and the differential (sample-to-reference) temperature measurements along the channel provide a measure of the heat capacity of the sample fluid. The differential temperature is thus influenced only by the difference in the energy-carrying capacity of the sample fluid vs. that of the reference fluid and is thus independent of other heat transfer modes. Initially, there is no flow, and a linear axial temperature gradient sets up along both channels in response to heat conduction, and any differential voltages from the thermocouples are recorded. This no-flow initialization provides a baseline and corrects for any deviations from symmetry (typically < 1 µV). With steady flow initiated in both channels, additional heat is transferred from hot to cold, and the quantity of advective heat transfer depends on the volumetric flowrate (Q), fluid density (), and specific heat capacity ( ). This advection will cause a deviation from the base temperature profile ( ∝ p ), shown in Fig. 1a . To determine the heat capacity, the flowrate of the sample channel may be adjusted to make the axial temperature profile match that of the reference (minimizing the temperature deviation, = ′). At this condition the specific heat capacity of the sample fluid ( p ′) can then be determined from the density () and flowrate (Q) via p ′ = p (/ ′ )(/ ′ ).\n\n...\n\nCompared with established values in the literature, 45 and the mean error for all fluids measured is ±1.1%. This level of accuracy is competitive with that of the best reference data available, and that of the gold standard method, DSC (typically Please do not adjust margins Please do not adjust margins 3-5% 46, 47 and in some cases as low as 1-2% 48, 49 ). For four liquids with greater discrepancies (Formamide, Glycerol, Ethylene Glycol, Octane in Table 1 ), additional C testing with a commercial DSC (Texas Instrument DSC 250) was performed.\n\n...\n\nThe microthermometric approach presented here can measure the heat capacity of fluids with accuracy matching that of the gold standard approach, in a manner that is two orders of magnitude faster and offers continuous flow-through operation. These capabilities bring calorimetry into the high throughput experimental workflows needed to accelerate the discovery of fluids and materials for the energy transition. 55 1. Svobodova, K. et al. Decarbonization, population disruption and resource inventories in the global energy transition. Nature Communications 2022 13:1 13, 1–16 (2022). 2. Hao, Z., Barecka, M. H. & Lapkin, A. A. Accelerating net zero from the perspective of optimizing a carbon capture and utilization system. Energy Environ Sci 15, 2139–2153 (2022). 3. Sinton, D. Energy: the microfluidic frontier. Lab Chip 14, 3127–3134 (2014). 4. Datta, S. S. et al. Lab on a chip for a low-carbon future. Lab Chip 23, 1358–1375 (2023). 5. Tijani, A. S. & Sudirman, A. S. bin. Thermos-physical properties and heat transfer characteristics of water/anti freezing and Al2O3/CuO based nanofluid as a coolant for car radiator. Int J Heat Mass Transf 118, 48–57 (2018). 6. Rodríguez-Martínez, X., Pascual-San-José, E. & Campoy Quiles, M. Accelerating organic solar cell material’s discovery: high-throughput screening and big data. Energy Environ Sci 14, 3301–3322 (2021). 7. An, N. G., Kim, J. Y. & Vak, D. Machine learning-assisted development of organic photovoltaics via high-throughput in situ formulation. Energy Environ Sci 14, 3438–3446 (2021). 8. Yoshii, Y. et al. High-throughput screening with nanoimprinting 3D culture for efficient drug development by mimicking the tumor environment. Biomaterials 51, 278–289 (2015). 9. Christensen, M. et al. Automation isn’t automatic. Chem Sci 12, 15473–15490 (2021). 10. Volk, A. A. et al. AlphaFlow: autonomous discovery and optimization of multi-step chemistry using a self-driven fluidic lab guided by reinforcement learning. Nat Commun 14, 1403 (2023). 11. Abolhasani, M. & Kumacheva, E. The rise of self-driving labs in chemical and materials sciences. Nature Synthesis 2, 483–492 (2023). 12. Lilley, D., Yu, P., Ma, J., Jain, A. & Prasher, R. Thermal fluids with high specific heat capacity through reversible Diels-Alder reactions. iScience 25, 103540 (2022).\n\n...\n\nWe introduced a differential microthermometric approach to calorimetry that circumvents the heat flux measurement that has limited incumbent methods. The differential nature of the measurement renders it independent of the heat flux, relying instead on the heat flux being similar between the test section and the reference. The result is a new approach to calorimetry -previously a slow, batch process -and one that offers accuracy competitive with the gold standard (±1.1%) and a twoorder-of-magnitude increase in measurement speed (∼1 minute vs. hours). The method enables fast measurements and was tested on a diverse set of fluids, including commercial and less common heat transfer fluids. These tests underscore the method's versatility and potential to support the rapid discovery of new fluids, or blends, that outperform. The method is insensitive to other fluid properties, broadly applicable to liquids and gases and capable of testing at elevated temperatures and pressures. The continuous flow-through nature of the measurement further enables integration within high-throughput experimental workflows. Also, in contrast to conventional calorimeters, the proposed differential microthermometric approach is insensitive to thermal conductivity. 40, 56 Accurate, reliable and high-rate measurement of specific heat capacity makes calorimetry compatible with high-throughput workflows and accelerates discovery of fluids and materials needed for the energy transition. A top priority of which is creating highperformance electrically insulating coolants for data centres, high energy density electric vehicle batteries, and electrification applications broadly.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Autonomous High‐Throughput Characterization of Liquid‐Liquid Phase Behavior\nURL: https://doi.org/10.1002/advs.76294\nPublished: 2026-06-30T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: T A Eid, Maryam Ebrahimiazar, Mohammad Zargartalebi, David Sinton\nHighlights:\nSelf-driving labs and data-driven formulation have outpaced the characterization of liquid-liquid miscibility and phase behavior, despite the role of such characterization in determining the stability and efficacy of complex formulations across diverse applications. Traditional characterization methods rely on labor-intensive visual inspection or single-proxy measurements that lack chemical generality, limit throughput, and provide only partial insight into phase behavior. Here, we report an automated platform that enables continuous, high-throughput screening of liquid-liquid phase behavior across diverse fluid chemistries. The device integrates an asymmetric capacitance sensor that is sensitive to the emergence and motion of phase boundaries, along with multi-angle turbidimetry that quantifies cloudiness and emulsion stability, in a single flow-through chamber. We demonstrate the classification of chemically diverse binary mixtures, resolution of real-time phase separation kinetics, and identification of partial miscibility across compositions and temperatures. For multicomponent systems, we employ Gaussian-process-based active learning to autonomously map ternary phase diagrams in ∼2 h, together with a nonlinear programming framework that extracts tie lines in ∼5 min per line. By unifying miscibility classification, kinetic characterization, and thermodynamic mapping in a single automated workflow, the platform enables comprehensive phase behavior screening at the scale and throughput required for autonomous formulation discovery.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Center for Foundation Models and AI Agents for Science\nURL: https://scifm.ai/projects/auto_lab.html\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n## Clio: An autonomous lab for battery electrolytes\n\nThe Clio self-driving lab integrates multiple experimental modules — including high-precision balances, viscometers, potentiostats, and peristaltic pumps — under a shared software framework, ElyteOS. Clio can characterize both aqueous and non-aqueous liquid formulations, and is a flow-through and modular setup, with flexibility to add additional instrumentation. It autonomously prepares electrolytes, performs electrochemical measurements, and characterizes their properties (density, conductivity and viscosity) without human supervision. Each experimental iteration is logged, version-controlled, and automatically stored in a cloud-accessible database.\n\n...\n\nThe SALSA solubility module complements Clio. It is an autonomous high-throughput solubility measurement system that captures real-time dissolution curves in aqueous and non-aqueous media. SALSA can rapidly evaluate solubility in mixed solvents, which can then be combined to predict the solution synthesis conditions of higher component materials. In combination, Clio and SALSA constitute a modular self-driving lab ecosystem capable of measuring conductivity, viscosity, density, surface tension, and solubility—core physical properties essential for electrolyte design.\n\n---\n\nTitle: SALSA: a low-cost self-driving lab modular add-on for salt solubility assessment for battery electrolytes  - Digital Discovery (RSC Publishing) DOI:10.1039/D5DD00516G\nURL: https://pubs.rsc.org/en/content/articlehtml/2026/dd/d5dd00516g\nPublished: 2026-03-31T06:53:38.000Z\nAuthor: Tianyi Zhang, Hongyi Lin, Yuhan Chen, Venkatasubramanian Viswanathan\nHighlights:\nSolubility is the maximum amount of solutes that can dissolve in a certain amount of solvent at a ... SDL) modular add-on for automated solubility measurement of liquid electrolytes, enabling automated liquid dosing, powder dosing, weighing, stirring,\n\n---\n\nTitle: Autonomous High‐Throughput Characterization of Liquid‐Liquid Phase Behavior\nURL: https://utoronto.scholaris.ca/items/b138750b-8517-423c-8bdd-bb5fcd77db6e/full\nPublished: 2026-06-30T00:42:21.000Z\nAuthor: Eid, Tarek, Ebrahimiazar, Maryam, Zargartalebi, Mohammad, Sinton, David\nHighlights:\n| dc.contributor.author | Eid, Tarek | |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| dc.contributor.author | Ebrahimiazar, Maryam | |\n| dc.contributor.author | Zargartalebi, Mohammad | |\n| dc.contributor.author | Sinton, David | |\n| dc.date.accessioned | 2026-07-29T19:00:28Z | |\n| dc.date.issued | 2026-06-30 | |\n| dc.description.abstract | Self-driving labs and data-driven formulation have outpaced the characterization of liquid-liquid miscibility and phase behavior, despite the role of such characterization in determining the stability and efficacy of complex formulations across diverse applications. Traditional characterization methods rely on labor-intensive visual inspection or single-proxy measurements that lack chemical generality, limit throughput, and provide only partial insight into phase behavior. Here, we report an automated platform that enables continuous, high-throughput screening of liquid-liquid phase behavior across diverse fluid chemistries. The device integrates an asymmetric capacitance sensor that is sensitive to the emergence and motion of phase boundaries, along with multi-angle turbidimetry that quantifies cloudiness and emulsion stability, in a single flow-through chamber. We demonstrate the classification of chemically diverse binary mixtures, resolution of real-time phase separation kinetics, and identification of partial miscibility across compositions and temperatures. For multicomponent systems, we employ Gaussian-process-based active learning to autonomously map ternary phase diagrams in ∼2 h, together with a nonlinear programming framework that extracts tie lines in ∼5 min per line. By unifying miscibility classification, kinetic characterization, and thermodynamic mapping in a single automated workflow, the platform enables comprehensive phase behavior screening at the scale and throughput required for autonomous formulation discovery. | |\n| dc.description.sponsorship | The authors gratefully acknowledge financial support from the Natural Sciences and Engineering Research Council of Canada (NSERC) via the Discovery Program (RGPIN-2025-06618), and the University of Toronto's Acceleration Consortium, which receives funding from the Canada First Research Excellence Fund (CFREF-2022-00042). This research was also undertaken, in part, thanks to the Canada Research Chairs Program (CRC-2021-00316). D. Sinton holds a Canada Research Chair in Energy and Fluids. | |\n| dc.identifier.citation | T. Eid, M. Ebrahimiazar, M. Zargartalebi, and D. Sinton, “ Autonomous High-Throughput Characterization of Liquid-Liquid Phase Behavior.” Advanced Science (2026): e76294. https://doi.org/10.1002/advs.76294 | |\n| dc.identifier.doi | 10.1002/advs.76294 | |\n| dc.identifier.issn | 2198-3844 | |\n| dc.identifier.issn | 2198-3844 | |\n| dc.identifier.uri | https://hdl.handle.net/1807/153435 | |\n| dc.language.iso | en | |\n| dc.publication.journal | Advanced Science | |\n| dc.publisher | Wiley | |\n| dc.rights | Attribution 4.0 International | en |\n| dc.rights.uri | http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ | |\n| dc.title | Autonomous High‐Throughput Characterization of Liquid‐Liquid Phase Behavior | |\n| dc.type | Article | |"
