    "content": "Title: Metric-based evaluation of electric vehicle battery ...\nURL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2352152X25037491\nPublished: 2025-11-07T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: Hanie Rezaei, Mahyar Rajabi Kochi, Maryam Ebrahimiazar, Mohammad Zargartalebi, Seyed Mohamad Moosavi, David A. Sinton\nHighlights:\n# Metric-based evaluation of electric vehicle battery immersion coolants\n\nJournal of Energy Storage. Published: 2025-11-07. 4 citations.\n\n## Authors\n\n- Hanie Rezaei\n- Mahyar Rajabi Kochi: h-index 4; 85 citations\n- Maryam Ebrahimiazar: h-index 4; 32 citations\n- Mohammad Zargartalebi: h-index 16; 1,170 citations\n- Seyed Mohamad Moosavi: h-index 31; 5,462 citations\n- David A. Sinton: h-index 113; 50,880 citations\n\n---\n\nTitle: Metric-based evaluation of electric vehicle battery immersion coolants - ADS\nURL: https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2025JEnSt.14019036R/abstract\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: Rezaei, Hanie\nHighlights:\nMetric-based evaluation of electric vehicle battery immersion coolants - ADS\n\n...\n\nNow on home page\n\n...\n\n## ADS\n\n|--\n\n## Metric-based evaluation of electric vehicle battery immersion coolants\n\n;\n\n;\n\n;\n\n;\n\n;\n\n- Sinton, David\n- Moosavi, Seyed Mohamad\n- Zargartalebi, Mohammad\n- Ebrahimiazar, Maryam\n- Rajabi-Kochi, Mahyar\n- Rezaei, Hanie\n\nThe widespread adoption of electric vehicles (EVs) depends on batteries with higher energy density and faster charging. Achieving these goals requires effective thermal management, with immersion cooling emerging as a promising solution. However, the lack of a standardized metric for screening immersion cooling fluids remains a challenge, in part because performance is a function of fluid properties and geometry. This study introduces a figure of merit (FOM) to quantify the thermal performance of dielectric fluids for the immersion cooling of the most common cylindrical EV battery packs. We combined computational fluid dynamics (CFD) and active learning techniques to screen over 1600 fluid mixtures drawn from liquid families potentially suitable for EV immersion cooling. Active learning drove the FOM with 15 data points—achieving a tenfold improvement in computational efficiency compared to random sampling. The resulting model was predictive (R2 = 0.99) across diverse dielectric fluids and binary mixtures, with silicon oils and essential oils achieving the highest FOM values. This work provides a metric-driven approach to rapidly screening and ranking fluids, facilitating the accelerated discovery of high-performance thermal fluids tailored to specific EV applications.\n\nPublication:\n\nJournal of Energy Storage\n\nPub Date: December 2025 DOI:\n\n10.1016/j.est.2025.119036\n\nBibcode: 2025JEnSt.14019036R Keywords:\n\n- Active learning\n- Computational fluid dynamics (CFD);\n- Figure of merit (FOM);\n- Immersion cooling;\n- Electric vehicles (EVs);\n\n---\n\nTitle: David Sinton | Scholarly & creative works | University of Toronto\nURL: https://discover.research.utoronto.ca/12742-david-sinton/publications\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nDiscovery\n\n---\n\nTitle: For a while now, we kept running into the same question: how do you fairly compare fluids for EV battery immersion cooling?\nURL: https://www.linkedin.com/posts/hanie-rezaei-76650978_for-a-while-now-we-kept-running-into-the-activity-7389714036692566016-N_NH\nPublished: 2025-10-30T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n# For a while now, we kept running into the same question: how do you fairly compare fluids for EV battery immersion cooling? · 2025-10-30T00:00:00+00:00\n\nFor a while now, we kept running into the same question: how do you fairly compare fluids for EV battery immersion cooling?\n\nWe all want higher-energy, faster-charging batteries—and that means more heat! Immersion cooling is promising, but picking the right coolant isn’t simple because performance depends on both the fluid and the pack geometry.\nSo we built a way to make the comparison honest and fast!\nIn our new paper, we introduce a figure of merit (FOM): a geometry-aware metric for the cylindrical EV battery packs. We paired CFD with active learning and screened 1,600+ fluid mixtures across families that could work for immersion cooling. The aim is straightforward: rank fluids quickly and get to better thermal designs sooner!\n\nI’m deeply grateful to Dr. David Sinton and Dr. Mohammad Zargartalebi for mentorship, to Dr. Mohamad Moosavi and his team for generous collaboration and support, and to my teammates at the Sinton Lab for creating such a supportive, energizing space. Thank you all!\n\nWorking on immersion cooling? Interested in the FOM—or tailoring it to new geometries and applications? Let’s connect.\n\nLink to paper: https: //https://lnkd.in/gRqZYjyw\n\n---\n\nTitle: Check out this work by Hanie Rezaei, who connects device-level modelling with Mahyar Rajabi Kochi's active learning in chemical space to develop a metric for ranking coolants for electric vehicles. We need … | Mohamad Moosavi\nURL: https://www.linkedin.com/posts/s-moh-moosavi_materialsdiscovery-cfd-ai4materials-activity-7389862910199590913-6mHx\nPublished: 2025-10-31T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: Mohamad Moosavi\nHighlights:\nCheck out this work by Hanie Rezaei, who connects device-level modelling with Mahyar Rajabi Kochi's active learning in chemical space to develop a metric for ranking coolants for electric vehicles. We need to see more of these [engineering + chemistry = chemical engineering] works to better understand and evaluate materials at the device/process level, while doing materials discovery!\n\n#materialsdiscovery #CFD #AI4Materials #AI4Chemistry #AI4Engineering For a while now, we kept running into the same question: how do you fairly compare fluids for EV battery immersion cooling?\n\nWe all want higher-energy, faster-charging batteries—and that means more heat! Immersion cooling is promising, but picking the right coolant isn’t simple because performance depends on both the fluid and the pack geometry.\nSo we built a way to make the comparison honest and fast!\nIn our new paper, we introduce a figure of merit (FOM): a geometry-aware metric for the cylindrical EV battery packs. We paired CFD with active learning and screened 1,600+ fluid mixtures across families that could work for immersion cooling. The aim is straightforward: rank fluids quickly and get to better thermal designs sooner!\n\nI’m deeply grateful to Dr. David Sinton and Dr. Mohammad Zargartalebi for mentorship, to Dr. Mohamad Moosavi and his team for generous collaboration and support, and to my teammates at the Sinton Lab for creating such a supportive, energizing space. Thank you all!\n\nWorking on immersion cooling? Interested in the FOM—or tailoring it to new geometries and applications? Let’s connect.\n\nLink to ... : https: // ... ://lnkd.in/gRqZYjyw\n\n---\n\nTitle: Screening biodegradable alternatives to mineral oil coolants\nURL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0196890425003711\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nIdentifying suitable alternatives involves screening various thermal fluids, a process that is inherently time-consuming due to the slow dynamics of heat and mass transfer. The most critical properties for cooling applications are viscosity, specific heat capacity, thermal conductivity, and density, as they define convective heat transfer efficiency through the Nusselt (Nu), Reynolds (Re), and Prandtl (Pr) numbers [1,43]. The Mouromtseff number [43] is a figure of merit that combines these properties and is used to rank thermal fluids [14,40,41,44]. The conventional measurement of these properties involves independent batch tests that require substantial volume (∼ 1–2 mL) and take between 20 min (e.g., for viscosity) and 4 h (e.g., for heat capacity) to conduct. The combined experimental cost of a screening campaign, with these existing approaches, is prohibitive.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Department of Chemical Engineering and Applied Chemistry\nURL: https://utoronto.scholaris.ca/collections/98bf73af-163c-4a47-9122-f1fc34bfdc50\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nChemical Engineering and Applied Chemistry at the University of Toronto is one of the oldest and largest departments of its kind in North America. It has ... history dating back to John Dalton of Dalton's Atomic Theory and a modern reputation based on its\n\n---\n\nTitle: Xitoy Tubular Bowl Centrifuge ishlab chiqaruvchilar etkazib beruvchilar fabrikasi\nURL: https://srcyrl.senz-separation-es.com/separator/tubular-centrifuge/high-speed-tubular-bowl-oil-centrifuge-for.html\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nchamber. Solids are retained, while liquid components are expelled from the top of the chamber through a collection tray. For liquid-liquid separations, two collection trays are used. The retained solids cake is dry and densely packed by the high"
